    <node id="&quot;HYPERPLASTIC POLYPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon that rarely turn into cancer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths on the colon or rectum lining that rarely turn into cancer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths that rarely turn into cancer, unlike adenomas."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAMILY MEMBERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Blood relatives whose health history is relevant for assessing genetic risk, including parents, siblings, children, and extended family."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members are blood relatives whose health history is relevant for assessing inherited cancer risk."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members are individuals who can provide support during cancer diagnosis and treatment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members are individuals who can provide support such as transportation, meals, and emotional support during FL treatment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members are part of a support system that can help with tasks and emotional support during cancer care."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members are part of a support system, providing transportation, meals, and emotional support."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members are relatives who can offer support, accompany patients to appointments, and help with daily tasks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members include siblings, half-siblings, aunts, uncles, nieces, nephews, grandparents, and grandchildren, whose health history is relevant for assessing cancer risk."&lt;SEP&gt;"Family members may carry mutations, so sharing family history is crucial for risk assessment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTROGEN RECEPTOR (ER)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CATEGORY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A specific type of hormone receptor that binds estrogen, providing survival and proliferation signals to breast cancer cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"Estrogen Receptor (ER) is a biomarker status that indicates whether the cancer cells have receptors for estrogen, which is important for prognosis and treatment decisions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Estrogen receptor (ER) is a protein on cancer cells that influences treatment decisions, with endocrine therapy targeting ER-positive cancers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Estrogen receptor is a protein found on some cancer cells that, when present, indicates the cancer may respond to hormone therapy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
